[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Honey-Bee (''Apis mellifica''), typical of ''Apidæ''. 1, queen; 2, neuter worker; 3, drone. (Slightly reduced.)}}}}
with the mouth-parts short and stout, as distinguished from the other bees, or ''Andrenidæ'', which have a long trunk. {{smaller|The family contains ''Apis'' (the hive-bees), ''Bombus'' (the bumblebees), and many other genera of social bees, besides a number of solitary ones, as ''Xylocopa'' (the carpenter-bees), etc. See ''bee'', ''Apis''<sup>1</sup>, and cuts under ''Anthophora'' and ''carpenter-bee''.}}

'''Apidæ'''<sup>2</sup> (ap′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Apus'' + ''-idæ''.] Same as ''Apodidæ''.

'''apiece''' (a̤-pēs′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' [Early mod. E. also ''apeece'', ''apece'', ''a piece'', ''a peece'', ''a pece'', < ME. ''a pece'': ''a'', E. ''a''<sup>3</sup>; ''pece'', piece; the prep. (''a''<sup>3</sup>) being merged in popular apprehension with the article (''a''<sup>2</sup>) and the noun extended in meaning: see ''a''<sup>3</sup>, ''a''<sup>2</sup>, and ''piece''.] For each piece, article, thing, or person; for each; to each; each: as, they cost a dollar ''apiece''; there is an orange ''apiece''.
{{smaller block|Neither have two coats ''apiece''.
{{right|Luke ix. 3.}}
<poem>In earnest, pray, how many men ''apiece''
Have you two been the death of?</poem>
{{right|''Ford'', Broken Heart, i. 2.}}}}

'''apieces†''' (a̤-pē′sez), ''adv.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''pieces''.] In or to pieces.

