'''apiin''' (ā′pi-in), ''n.'' [< L. ''apium'', parsley, + ''-in''<sup>2</sup>.] A gelatinous substance obtained from common

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller|detachments of other genera limited to the hive-bee (''Apis mellifica'') and its immediate relatives. See ''bee'', and cut under ''Apidæ''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''Apis'''<sup>2</sup> (ā′pis), ''n.'' [L., < Gr. Ἆπις, < Egypt. ''Hapi'', lit. ‘hidden.’] The sacred bull of the ancient Egyptians, to which divine honors were paid. {{smaller|The bull sought out by the priests for this purpose was required to be black with a triangular white spot on the forehead, and with numerous other marks which denoted the true Apis.}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Apis.<br>Mummy in the collection of the New York Historical Society.}}}}

'''apish''' (ā′pish), ''a.'' [< ''ape'' + ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] Having the qualities of an ape; inclined to imitate in a servile manner; hence, foolishly foppish, affected, or trifling: as, ''apish'' manners.
{{smaller block|A kinde of birds as it were of an ''apish'' kinde, ready to imitate what they see done.
{{right|''Holland'', tr. of Camden’s Britannia (1637), p. 543.}}}}

'''apishamore''' (a-pish′ạ-mōr), ''n.'' [Origin not ascertained.] In the western United States, a saddle-blanket made of the skin of a buffalo-calf.

'''apishly''' (ā′pish-li), ''adv.'' In an apish manner; with silly imitation; foppishly.
{{smaller block|Sin is so ''apishly'' crafty, as to hide itself under the colours and masks of goodness and honesty.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Artif. Handsomeness, p. 15.}}}}

'''apishness''' (ā′pish-nes), ''n.'' [< ''apish'' + ''-ness.''] The quality of being apish; mimicry; foppery: as, “the ''apishness'' of foreign manners,” ''Warburton'', Sermons.

